 just terrible alone. I don't want to break the king's pair, it will be a good defense and attack move later.\n\n**Re-Evaluating Card Strength & Long-Term Strategy:**\n\nOkay, playing the straight does that. I think this play makes sense, because I have the Rocket and the Bomb as backup, but I don't want to use them just yet because I may need them later. Okay, straight 8-Q, is the move. I dump the garbage, and keep the good pairs.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand possesses invincible strength with the Rocket and a Bomb of 2s, but is cluttered with low pairs (33, 44) and several singles (5, 6, 8, 9, J, Q). The primary objective is to clear these weaker cards efficiently. Playing the straight from 8 to Q is the most optimal move as it consumes 5 cards, clearing four singles (8, 9, J, Q) and one 10, while crucially preserving the Pair of Kings. Keeping the Pair of Kings is superior to extending the straight to King (which would leave a weak Single King), as the Pair provides a strong defensive option to regain initiative.\n\nReason: This move maximizes hand efficiency by grouping multiple singles into a 5-card sequence, reducing the number of turns required to empty the hand. It preserves the strong Pair of Kings for future control, maintaining a balanced hand structure supported by the Rocket and Bomb to close out the game.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣8", "♠9", "♥10", "♥J", "♠Q"]}'}
